//! lotus/reverb_pan.rye -- ALES213: REVERB PAN -- reverberate the master WET, then place the reverberated result WHERE it sits in the field, the
//! next rung after ALES212 closed HOW WIDE the wash sits. Width answered how wide; pan answers where. A keeper wants the reverb return pushed toward
//! one side while the dry mix holds centre -- a wash panned left or right -- and that is exactly "reverberate, then pan the wet image".
//!
//! The shape, composing three proven stages with NO new audio arithmetic:
//!   1. validate the pan fraction BEFORE any write (a zero or over-bound denominator, or a position past the field, refuses `BadPan`)
//!   2. reverberate the master WET in place -- ALES191's `stereo_reverb_preset`, unchanged (it prechecks its own faults before any write)
//!   3. read ALES11's equal-power split `[w_left, w_right]` for the position, and scale each wet channel by its weight over the field denominator
//!      through ALES2's proven `timeline.gain` -- the pan already proven legal so neither gain can fault
//! The pan is checked first so a bad position never lets the reverb write: the op refuses whole up front (the master left dry) or completes whole.
//!
//! The edges name the knob. `pan == 0` (HARD LEFT) gives weights [den, 0]: the left channel is scaled by den/den (identity) and the right by 0/den
//! (silence), so the whole wet wash routes to the LEFT and the right goes quiet. `pan == den` (HARD RIGHT) mirrors it -- the wash routes wholly RIGHT.
//! `pan == den/2` (CENTRE) gives equal weights isqrt(den²/2) ≈ 0.707-den on both sides: the EQUAL-POWER centre holds the wash's loudness (−3 dB per
//! channel) rather than the linear −6 dB, so a centred wet stays centred and both channels are attenuated equally below the pure wet. Because each
//! weight is a non-negative amplitude no greater than den (ALES11's isqrt is monotone, den² its cap), each channel is a fraction of the wet, so the
//! gain's saturate is a proven NO-OP -- the pan attenuates, never amplifies.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES213.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-123603_fill-ales213-lotus-reverb-pan.md
//! Stands on: lotus/stereo_reverb_preset.rye (ALES191) -- the named-room stereo wet, run over the master unchanged, its StereoReverbPresetError
//! forwarded whole; lotus/power.rye (ALES11) -- the equal-power split `split(pos, den)` = [isqrt(den-(den−pos)), isqrt(den-pos)], the two amplitude
//! weights on the quarter circle, reused verbatim; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip and its proven `gain` (each wet channel scaled by a
//! fraction, saturated once); lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip, its balance invariant, and the fold bound `max_pan_den`; lotus/clock.rye
//! (ALES5) -- the validated sample clock the preset speaks its span against.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. One bounded in-process i16 StereoClip, a validated clock, ALES191's fixed banks, siloed to lotus/.
//! Reverb pan is a pure composition of proven stages over existing samples; it fabricates none, changes no length, and reads no byte past either
//! channel. The pan law is a routing weight, not a psychoacoustic or electrical claim. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real
//! device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/reverb_pan.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const clock = @import("clock.rye");
const stereo_reverb_preset = @import("stereo_reverb_preset.rye");
const power = @import("power.rye");

pub const reverb_pan_version = "20260815.123603";

/// The named rooms are ALES190's own, reached through ALES191 -- reused whole, so reverb pan and the presets name the same tunings and cannot drift
/// apart.
pub const Room = stereo_reverb_preset.Room;

/// The widest pan denominator this rung admits is ALES10's own fold bound, reached through ALES11 -- reused whole, so reverb pan and the plain pan
/// name the same field and cannot drift apart. A pan is `pos/den`: 0 is hard left, `den` is hard right, `den/2` is the equal-power centre.
pub const max_pan_den: u32 = power.max_pan_den;

/// Reverb pan's faults are ALES191's `StereoReverbPresetError` (the clock's DurationTooLong and the reverb's own faults, forwarded whole) beside one
/// own fault, `BadPan` -- a pan with a zero or over-bound denominator or a position past the field, refused by name before any write.
pub const ReverbPanError = stereo_reverb_preset.StereoReverbPresetError || error{BadPan};

/// Reverberate `count_ms` of a stereo master `sc` from `start_ms` with a NAMED ROOM, then place the reverberated result at `pan_pos/pan_den` in the
/// field by scaling each wet channel by its equal-power weight. `pan_pos == 0` routes the whole wash to the LEFT (right silenced); `pan_pos ==
/// pan_den` routes it to the RIGHT; `pan_pos == pan_den/2` is the equal-power centre (both channels ≈ 0.707-wet, the wash held centred at its
/// loudness). Refuses BadPan on a bad fraction BEFORE the reverb writes, then forwards ALES191's StereoReverbPresetError (DurationTooLong on a span
/// past the clip, BadRange on an out-of-range span) before any write; on refusal the master is left dry, untouched, and balanced.
pub fn reverb_pan(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    clk: clock.Clock,
    room: Room,
    start_ms: u32,
    count_ms: u32,
    pan_pos: u32,
    pan_den: u32,
) ReverbPanError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant the shared preset and gains preserve.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    // invariant: the clock is valid -- the span is spoken against it, and stereo_reverb_preset asserts the same.
    assert(clk.rate >= 1 and clk.rate <= clock.max_rate);
    // invariant: the pan is a real, bounded fraction -- checked BEFORE the reverb writes so a bad pan never leaves a wet, un-placed master. A zero
    // denominator is no fraction at all; a position past the field is no place in it; the denominator is bounded to ALES11's fold bound so the
    // equal-power split stays within its proven range.
    if (pan_den == 0 or pan_den > max_pan_den or pan_pos > pan_den) return error.BadPan;

    const n: u32 = sc.left.len;

    // 1. Reverberate the master WET in place -- ALES191's stereo_reverb_preset, unchanged. It prechecks DurationTooLong / BadRange / its own reverb
    //    faults before any write, so a reverb fault here leaves the master dry and balanced (and the pan already proven legal above).
    try stereo_reverb_preset.stereo_reverb_preset(sc, clk, room, start_ms, count_ms);

    // 2. Read ALES11's equal-power split for the proven-legal position -- [w_left, w_right], each a non-negative amplitude ≤ pan_den (isqrt monotone,
    //    pan_den² its cap). No pan arithmetic is re-derived here; the split is spent verbatim.
    const w = power.split(pan_pos, pan_den);
    // invariant: each weight is a legal attenuation numerator -- no greater than the field denominator, so each channel gain is a fraction of the wet
    //   and the gain's saturate is a proven no-op (the pan attenuates, never amplifies).
    assert(w[0] <= pan_den and w[1] <= pan_den);

    // 3. Scale each wet channel by its weight over the field denominator -- ALES2's proven gain, saturating once. The span is the full valid clip and
    //    the denominator is proven non-zero above, so neither gain can raise BadGain or BadRange; the catch restores nothing new and re-raises as our
    //    own fault, yet the invariant is that it never fires.
    timeline.gain(&sc.left, 0, n, @intCast(w[0]), pan_den) catch {
        // invariant: unreachable -- the pan is prechecked legal and the span is the whole clip, so gain cannot fault here.
        return error.BadPan;
    };
    timeline.gain(&sc.right, 0, n, @intCast(w[1]), pan_den) catch {
        // invariant: unreachable -- as above, for the right channel.
        return error.BadPan;
    };

    // invariant: reverb pan writes values only -- both channels keep their length, so the master leaves balanced.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len and sc.left.len == n);
}

// --- selftest: THE HARD-LEFT LAW (pan == 0) -- the left channel is the pure wet left byte-exact, the right silenced; THE HARD-RIGHT LAW (pan == den)
//     -- the mirror; THE COMPOSITION LAW (an off-centre pan) -- reverb_pan equals stereo_reverb_preset then the two equal-power gains byte for byte, and
//     each channel is an attenuation of the wet; THE CENTRE-SYMMETRY LAW -- a centred master reverberates to a centred wet, and the equal-power centre
//     gives equal weights, so panning it to centre keeps left == right, each attenuated; THE FAULT LAW -- a bad pan refuses BadPan, a span past the
//     clip DurationTooLong, an out-of-range span BadRange, each leaving the master dry and untouched ------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn abs16(x: i16) i32 {
    const v: i32 = x;
    return if (v < 0) -v else v;
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const c = try clock.make(1000); // 1 sample per ms — each ms delay is one sample, the bench legible
    // Two distinct impulses so the wet channels genuinely differ (no crossing can hide), each long enough for the hall's strongest comb to decay.
    const left = blk: {
        var b: [1024]i16 = [_]i16{0} ** 1024;
        b[0] = 12000;
        break :blk b;
    };
    const right = blk: {
        var b: [1024]i16 = [_]i16{0} ** 1024;
        b[3] = 9000; // a different impulse at a different position — the right channel is not the left
        break :blk b;
    };

    // The pure stereo reverb -- the wet the pan reshapes. Computed once so every edge reads against the same wash.
    var wet = pan.StereoClip{};
    try load_stereo(&wet, &left, &right);
    try stereo_reverb_preset.stereo_reverb_preset(&wet, c, .hall, 0, 1024);
    const wl = wet.left.samples();
    const wr = wet.right.samples();

    // 1. THE HARD-LEFT LAW -- pan == 0 gives weights [den, 0]: the left channel is scaled by den/den (identity) so it is byte-exact the pure wet left,
    //    and the right by 0/den (silence) so it is all zero. The whole wash routes left.
    {
        var clip = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&clip, &left, &right);
        try reverb_pan(&clip, c, .hall, 0, 1024, 0, 2);
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), wl)) return error.HardLeftNotWet;
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < wr.len) : (i += 1) if (clip.right.samples()[i] != 0) return error.HardLeftRightNotSilent;
    }

    // 2. THE HARD-RIGHT LAW -- pan == den gives weights [0, den]: the right channel is byte-exact the pure wet right, the left all zero. The mirror.
    {
        var clip = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&clip, &left, &right);
        try reverb_pan(&clip, c, .hall, 0, 1024, 2, 2);
        if (!eq(clip.right.samples(), wr)) return error.HardRightNotWet;
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < wl.len) : (i += 1) if (clip.left.samples()[i] != 0) return error.HardRightLeftNotSilent;
    }

    // 3. THE COMPOSITION LAW -- an off-centre pan with a wide denominator: reverb_pan IS reverberate-then-place, so its result equals ALES191's reverb
    //    over a fresh copy, then ALES11's equal-power gains, BYTE FOR BYTE on both channels (the composition proof, no new arithmetic); and each
    //    channel is an attenuation of the pure wet (each weight ≤ den, so the gain never amplifies).
    {
        var clip = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&clip, &left, &right);
        try reverb_pan(&clip, c, .hall, 0, 1024, 1000, 4096); // off-centre, wide denominator (truncation lives here)

        // the reference: the pure reverb, then the two equal-power gains -- exactly the stages reverb_pan composes.
        var ref = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&ref, &left, &right);
        try stereo_reverb_preset.stereo_reverb_preset(&ref, c, .hall, 0, 1024);
        const w = power.split(1000, 4096);
        try timeline.gain(&ref.left, 0, ref.left.len, @intCast(w[0]), 4096);
        try timeline.gain(&ref.right, 0, ref.right.len, @intCast(w[1]), 4096);

        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), ref.left.samples())) return error.CompositionLeftBroken;
        if (!eq(clip.right.samples(), ref.right.samples())) return error.CompositionRightBroken;

        // each channel is an attenuation of the pure wet -- the pan places, it does not amplify.
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < wl.len) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(clip.left.samples()[i]) > abs16(wl[i])) return error.LeftAmplified;
            if (abs16(clip.right.samples()[i]) > abs16(wr[i])) return error.RightAmplified;
        }
    }

    // 4. THE CENTRE-SYMMETRY LAW -- a centred master (left == right) reverberates through one shared bank to a centred wet (left == right); the
    //    equal-power centre gives equal weights (isqrt(den²/2) on both sides), so panning it to centre keeps the two channels identical, each
    //    attenuated below the pure wet.
    {
        var centred_wet = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&centred_wet, &left, &left);
        try stereo_reverb_preset.stereo_reverb_preset(&centred_wet, c, .hall, 0, 1024);
        const cwl = centred_wet.left.samples();

        var clip = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&clip, &left, &left);
        try reverb_pan(&clip, c, .hall, 0, 1024, 2048, 4096); // centre
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), clip.right.samples())) return error.CentreNotSymmetric;
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < cwl.len) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(clip.left.samples()[i]) > abs16(cwl[i])) return error.CentreNotAttenuated;
        }
    }

    // 5. THE FAULT LAW -- a zero denominator, an over-bound denominator, and a position past the field each refuse BadPan BEFORE the reverb writes, so
    //    the master is left DRY, untouched, and balanced; a count past max_clip forwards DurationTooLong; an out-of-range span forwards BadRange.
    {
        var clip = pan.StereoClip{};
        const threel = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        const threer = [_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 };
        try load_stereo(&clip, &threel, &threer);
        // a zero denominator is no pan -- BadPan, before the reverb writes, the master left dry.
        if (reverb_pan(&clip, c, .room, 0, 3, 1, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroDenSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPan) return error.WrongZeroDen;
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), &threel) or !eq(clip.right.samples(), &threer)) return error.ZeroDenMutated;
        // an over-bound denominator -- BadPan, master dry.
        if (reverb_pan(&clip, c, .room, 0, 3, 1, max_pan_den + 1)) |_| return error.OverDenSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPan) return error.WrongOverDen;
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), &threel) or !eq(clip.right.samples(), &threer)) return error.OverDenMutated;
        // a position past the field -- BadPan, master dry.
        if (reverb_pan(&clip, c, .room, 0, 3, 3, 2)) |_| return error.OverPosSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPan) return error.WrongOverPos;
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), &threel) or !eq(clip.right.samples(), &threer)) return error.OverPosMutated;
        // a count past max_clip converts past the clip bound -- DurationTooLong from the clock, before any write.
        if (reverb_pan(&clip, c, .room, 0, timeline.max_clip + 1, 1, 2)) |_| return error.LongSpanSlipped else |e| if (e != error.DurationTooLong) return error.WrongLongSpan;
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), &threel) or !eq(clip.right.samples(), &threer)) return error.LongSpanMutated;
        // an out-of-range span (2 ms start, 5 ms count past a 3-sample clip) refuses BadRange before any write.
        if (reverb_pan(&clip, c, .room, 2, 5, 1, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(clip.left.samples(), &threel) or !eq(clip.right.samples(), &threer)) return error.BadRangeMutated;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-reverb-pan: REVERB PAN — reverberate the master WET, then place the reverberated result WHERE it sits in the field, the next " ++
        "rung after ALES212 closed HOW WIDE the wash sits. A keeper wants the reverb return pushed toward one side while the dry mix holds centre — " ++
        "a wash panned left or right — that is exactly 'reverberate, then pan the wet image'. The shape composes three proven stages with NO new " ++
        "audio arithmetic: validate the pan first (a bad fraction refuses BadPan before the reverb writes), reverberate wet in place (ALES191's " ++
        "stereo_reverb_preset, unchanged), place the wet image (ALES11's equal-power split feeding ALES2's proven gain on each channel, the pan " ++
        "already proven legal so neither gain can fault). THE HARD-LEFT LAW: pan == 0 gives weights [den, 0], so the left channel is byte-exact the " ++
        "pure wet left and the right is silenced — the whole wash routes left. THE HARD-RIGHT LAW: pan == den mirrors it, the wash routing wholly " ++
        "right. THE COMPOSITION LAW: an off-centre pan equals the reverb then the two equal-power gains byte for byte, each channel an attenuation " ++
        "of the pure wet. THE CENTRE-SYMMETRY LAW: a centred master reverberates to a centred wet, and the equal-power centre gives equal weights " ++
        "(isqrt(den²/2), ≈ 0.707·den, −3 dB per channel), so panning it to centre keeps left == right, each attenuated below the wet. THE FAULT " ++
        "LAW: a bad pan refuses BadPan, a span past the clip DurationTooLong, an out-of-range span BadRange, each leaving the master DRY and " ++
        "untouched. Purely local: one StereoClip, a validated clock, ALES191's fixed banks; no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real " ++
        "speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
